.\" hx(1) -- man-macro reference page
.\" NOTE: this is the man page for the hx COMMAND.  The hx LANGUAGE
.\" specification is a separate mm-macro document of the same stem, kept in
.\" a different directory.  Neither is derived from the other.
.\" $Revision: 1.6 $
.\" $Log: hx.1,v $
.\" Revision 1.6  2026/09/25 16:23:16  dlr
.\" Document how a candidate is presented. The page named the bound variables but never said what pass is bound TO, so the one thing a caller must know to use hx as an oracle was absent: that a candidate may arrive wrapped and that the wrapper is decoded. New subsection under EXPRESSION LANGUAGE covers $HEX[] and $TESTVEC[], states that both are read on -p and on stdin and mean the same to hashpipe under -X and in verify mode, gives the vector grammar, notes the 2 MB bound on a decoded vector, records that a string which does not parse as either is taken literally rather than rejected, and points at fromhex() for hex that is not wrapped. Written after hx.c 1.5 made $TESTVEC[] work in this command at all -- it had been hex-decoding the pattern and dropping the repeat count -- and hashpipe.c 1.208 made both wrappers native on -X, so the page now describes behaviour that is actually uniform across the channels it claims.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.5  2026/09/04 17:16:55  dlr
.\" Wording in the revision notes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.4  2026/08/29 18:06:35  dlr
.\" Update the hashpipe -X note: it decodes $HEX[...] identically as of hashpipe.c 1.114, so the two surfaces of the language now agree on input. Keeps the pre-1.114 divergence as a single clause, since it explains output from older builds. The output asymmetry is unchanged and still documented: hx does not wrap on the way out, and a _bin expression writes raw bytes.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.3  2026/08/29 17:35:32  dlr
.\" Document the $HEX[] asymmetry, all verified by running. Input: -p and stdin lines decode $HEX[] and $TESTVEC[], shown with a pair of equivalent runs, and this is how a password containing a colon or a high-bit byte is supplied on a command line at all. Output: NOT wrapped -- a _bin expression writes raw bytes, unsafe to pipe into a line-oriented tool, so either encode inside the expression with hex() or wrap on the way out with rehex(1). Also notes that hashpipe -X does not perform the input decoding despite running the same language.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.2  2026/08/29 17:16:18  dlr
.\" Add BUILDING section per Waffle: hx is not distributed as a binary, only built from source. Documents make hx in-tree (the target exists but is deliberately absent from all, which is why a normal build does not produce the command) and a portable hand-build using $PREFIX rather than a hardcoded local prefix. Records the three ways the build goes wrong: HX_STANDALONE without HX_NO_MAIN, since defining both gates out main and yields a library-flavoured object; myprogress.c required or myprogress is undefined at link; and HX_HAS_KDF only with the KDF objects linked. Also states that options precede the expression, and adds a tested example with its output. Removes the redundant .PP after .SH EXAMPLES.
.\"
.\" Revision 1.1  2026/08/29 16:30:16  dlr
.\" Initial revision: hx(1) man page for the hx COMMAND. All 8 options plus the $HEX[] and $TESTVEC[] input decoding. Defers the language itself to the mm-macro hx specification, which stays authoritative.
.\"
.TH HX 1 "2026-08-29" "hx" "Password Recovery"
.SH NAME
hx \- evaluate hash expressions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hx
.RI [ options ]
.I expression
.br
.B hx
.RI [ options ]
.BI \-f " script.hx"
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hx
compiles and runs a program in the hx hash expression language: a notation
for stating exactly which cryptographic operations, in which order, turn a
password into a digest.
.PP
.B hx
is not distributed as a binary.  It is built from the source tree on demand;
see
.BR BUILDING .
.PP
The command is a calculator.  It computes and prints; it reads no hash file
and verifies nothing.  Its uses are working out what an unfamiliar hash
construction actually is, producing test vectors, and drafting an
expression before installing it as a user-defined type in
.BR userdef.txt ,
where
.BR mdxfind (1)
and
.BR hashpipe (1)
can search on it.
.PP
Options precede the
.IR expression ,
which is the sole non-option argument.  Either an
.I expression
or a
.B \-f
script is required, and not both.  Without
.BR \-p ,
passwords are read from standard input, one per line, and one result is
printed per input line.  Results are lowercase hexadecimal unless the
expression says otherwise.
.PP
A password argument or input line beginning with
.BI $HEX[ ... ]
is hex-decoded before use, as is
.BI $TESTVEC[ ... ]\fR,
which exists for large binary vectors and accepts up to 2 MB decoded.  Hex
decoding stops at the first non-hex character, so the closing bracket is
optional.  These two are therefore the same run:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hx \-p alpha 'md5(pass)'
2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9

$ hx \-p '$HEX[616c706861]' 'md5(pass)'
2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9
.fi
.RE
.PP
The wrapper is how a password containing a colon, a control character or a
high-bit byte is supplied on a command line at all.
.B hashpipe \-X
decodes it identically as of hashpipe.c 1.114; before that release it did
not, and hashed the wrapper as literal text.
.PP
Output is
.I not
wrapped.  A
.B _bin
expression writes raw bytes to standard output, which is unsafe to pipe into
a line-oriented tool; either encode inside the expression with
.BR hex (),
or wrap on the way out with
.BR rehex (1):
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hx \-p alpha 'md5_bin(pass)' | rehex
$HEX[2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9]
.fi
.RE
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " password"
Evaluate for this single password instead of reading standard input.
.TP
.BI \-f " file"
Read the program from
.I file
rather than the command line.
.TP
.BI \-s " salt"
Bind the
.B salt
variable.
.TP
.BI \-S " salt2"
Bind the
.B salt2
variable.
.TP
.BI \-P " pepper"
Bind the
.B pepper
variable.
.TP
.BI \-u " user"
Bind the
.B user
variable.
.TP
.B \-d
Dump the abstract syntax tree and exit without compiling further.
.TP
.B \-b
Dump the compiled bytecode and exit without running.
.TP
.B \-h
Print the usage summary and exit.
.SH BUILDING
No
.B hx
binary is shipped.  The source tree carries a target for it, so inside a
checkout the whole job is:
.PP
.RS
.nf
make hx
.fi
.RE
.PP
That target is deliberately absent from
.BR all ,
which is why a normal build does not produce the command.  To build it by
hand, or outside the tree:
.PP
.RS
.nf
cc \-DHX_STANDALONE \-O3 \-I. \-I$PREFIX/include \e
   \-o hx hx.c hx_ast.c hx_compile.c hx_vm.c hx_func.c \e
         hx.tab.c hx.lex.c myprogress.c \e
   \-L$PREFIX/lib \-lssl \-lcrypto $PREFIX/lib/libiconv.a
.fi
.RE
.PP
.I $PREFIX
is wherever OpenSSL and libiconv are installed \(em
.BR /usr ,
.BR /usr/local ,
.B /opt/local
and
.B /opt/homebrew
are the usual candidates, and the two need not share a prefix.
.PP
Three things go wrong if you deviate:
.TP 3
.B \(bu
Define
.B HX_STANDALONE
and
.I not
.BR HX_NO_MAIN .
The
.BR main ()
function is gated on
.B HX_STANDALONE
and the absence of
.BR HX_NO_MAIN ,
so defining both compiles the same sources into a library-flavoured object
with no entry point.  The in-tree codegen tooling defines both on purpose;
that recipe is not a template for building the command.
.TP
.B \(bu
.B myprogress.c
is required.  Omitting it leaves
.B myprogress
undefined at link time.
.TP
.B \(bu
Do not define
.B HX_HAS_KDF
unless you also link the KDF objects, or the link fails on
.BR crypto_scrypt ,
.BR yescrypt_r ,
.B yescrypt_init_local
and
.BR bsd_crypt_des .
Without it the binary builds and every hash primitive works; only the
password-based key derivation functions are absent.
.SH "EXPRESSION LANGUAGE"
The bound variables are
.BR pass ,
.BR salt ,
.BR salt2 ,
.B pepper
and
.BR user .
Concatenation is
.BR . ;
a hash function applied to an argument yields its lowercase hex by default;
the
.B _bin
suffix yields raw bytes instead; and
.BI ^ n
after a function name iterates it
.I n
times.
.SS "How the candidate is presented"
A candidate may be written wrapped, and
.B pass
is bound to the decoded bytes rather than to the wrapper text:
.PP
.RS
.TP 30
.BI $HEX[ hexdigits ]
the bytes those hex digits spell
.TP
.BI $TESTVEC[ "HH x N" ]
the byte pattern
.I HH
repeated
.I N
times
.RE
.PP
Both forms are read on
.B \-p
and on standard input, and mean the same to
.BR hashpipe (1)
under
.B \-X
and in verify mode, so one string gives one digest on every channel.
They exist because some passwords cannot be written literally: one holding a
colon, a newline or a non-UTF-8 byte needs
.BR $HEX[] ,
and a repeated vector of megabytes does not fit on a line at all without
.BR $TESTVEC[] .
In a vector the pattern is hex, the count decimal, the separator any run of
non-digits, and the closing bracket optional.
A decoded vector is bounded at 2 MB.
.PP
A string that does not parse as either form \(em unbalanced, not hex, or a
zero repeat count \(em is taken literally instead of rejected, so a password
that merely resembles a wrapper still hashes as itself.
Use
.B fromhex()
for hex that is not wrapped: it decodes an argument, where the wrappers
describe how the candidate itself arrived.
.PP
.RS
.TP 30
.B md5(pass)
plain MD5
.TP
.B sha1(md5(pass) . salt)
SHA1 over the MD5 hex, then the salt
.TP
.B md5_bin(pass)
raw 16-byte MD5 output
.TP
.B md5^1000(pass)
MD5 iterated 1000 times
.TP
.B hex(md5_bin(salt . pass))
explicit hex encoding of a binary result
.RE
.PP
The full language \(em every function, encoding, control construct and the
catalogued expression for each of the supported hash types \(em is
specified in the mm-macro hx reference document, which is the authority.
This page covers the command only.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
0 on success, 1 on a usage error, a compile failure, or an allocation
failure.
.SH EXAMPLES
The simplest possible run, showing the option-before-expression order and the
default lowercase-hex output:
.PP
.RS
.nf
$ hx \-p alpha 'md5(pass)'
2c1743a391305fbf367df8e4f069f9f9
.fi
.RE
.PP
Identify a construction by computing candidates for a known pair:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hx \-p password123 \-s 3f2a 'md5(salt . md5(pass))'
.fi
.RE
.PP
Generate vectors for a whole wordlist:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hx \-s deadbeef 'sha256(pass . salt)' < words.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Inspect what an expression compiles to before trusting it:
.PP
.RS
.nf
hx \-b 'sha1(md5(pass) . salt)'
.fi
.RE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR mdxfind (1),
.BR hashpipe (1)
.PP
.B hashpipe \-X
evaluates the same language with the full hashpipe function registry
available, and
.B hashpipe \-D
is the equivalent of
.BR "hx \-b" .
.SH AUTHOR
Waffle.
